#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dedcee-452d-50ff-844e-5648be3066b4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Amplitude of an Simple Harmonic Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8ddac9d-ef24-5514-be90-f3d733653b1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146480} {:answer/id #uuid "08f14a0c-4ef7-5673-a935-45ba89549676" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146481} {:answer/id #uuid "13e87419-0471-564e-926b-65e4be686ec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146482} {:answer/id #uuid "2f9dfb41-5d51-573d-abe2-fcb9c4d476d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146483} {:answer/id #uuid "3457954e-205e-55c6-a8aa-7ac21a6bc723" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3457954e-205e-55c6-a8aa-7ac21a6bc723" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146484} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c0a285f-ab33-5e4b-a533-958ef699ef73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35df61e1-761d-55a5-a2f5-02b52974f7d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-64218" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $2\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $2\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=2.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2R\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n2=2R\\,⟹\\,R=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $1\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dedcee-452d-50ff-844e-5648be3066b4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Amplitude of an Simple Harmonic Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98062e33-0dea-51f2-96db-92c1aa49b6a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382094} {:answer/id #uuid "9a6bb57e-e80d-579f-a04f-713b9a99a3a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382095} {:answer/id #uuid "da9558f3-4792-56ac-9f97-33600fcb73ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382096} {:answer/id #uuid "1374676a-8d6e-5aa5-8305-21473638d1c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382097} {:answer/id #uuid "da8d6bb4-728f-5cd9-8269-66e598d37822" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da9558f3-4792-56ac-9f97-33600fcb73ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382096} :answer-field/id #uuid "155056e4-6b01-569c-9f98-173a1e551d36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dcd4b923-bea2-5168-a3b2-3077cdbbb018" :question/math-academy-id "q-79494" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+81y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $3\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=81\\,⟹\\,\\omega =9.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $3\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=3.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=9R\\operatorname{cos}⁡9t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=3$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n3=9R\\,⟹\\,R=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e17fe7fc-3b85-5f2c-aa69-2336b05b16b2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Simple Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75466738-745c-5eb1-a2ef-f5ca9e1c3915" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382101} {:answer/id #uuid "2df1b856-55d8-52ce-a1a1-1f87a657c8ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382102} {:answer/id #uuid "4f96029f-2cb7-5400-bf61-9b113149ed9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382103} {:answer/id #uuid "3b9435ae-b2f1-5774-a3a2-0d1e42ec7c4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382104} {:answer/id #uuid "17be7e3a-a5d6-525e-b2df-f105798b2da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f96029f-2cb7-5400-bf61-9b113149ed9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382103} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa1cf199-0396-5cda-aee3-5066f446bb2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ff5f471e-8a86-5bf8-982b-2ec38a9cfd12" :question/math-academy-id "q-94275" :question/problem "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nTherefore, only function III could be used to represent $y(t).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Mass-Spring System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2838c98-1d1f-51a7-9301-0b95c70e13d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{6}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382108} {:answer/id #uuid "bb4712b3-9847-53df-9bbc-5b7d47195bb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{3}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382109} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f8097a-239f-5bdc-a054-5e5a695a7505" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+6y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382110} {:answer/id #uuid "37ae2556-09e2-51b4-95c8-1d13ff0c7c4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{2}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382111} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca27d28-8e16-5677-a575-fddb6d45e7d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+9y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1f8097a-239f-5bdc-a054-5e5a695a7505" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+6y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382110} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa783c74-d3a9-5a57-a529-880c8bfab050" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ccb94392-acad-518a-b317-b59ffe1a3a81" :question/math-academy-id "q-94286" :question/problem "A particle of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=18\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{18}{3}=6.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6y=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Mass-Spring System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "115cd997-b71b-55b2-87b1-321d1fc8e334" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382115} {:answer/id #uuid "338b903c-62dd-5ae2-86ca-830414dfe7cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382116} {:answer/id #uuid "9adf627e-e147-58a0-b5eb-ab1dbe8670cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382117} {:answer/id #uuid "7bbadcd2-446b-58bb-a5dc-944482bffeb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382118} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a7c864-ec43-5073-8381-13050f5d4620" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9adf627e-e147-58a0-b5eb-ab1dbe8670cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382117} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6d0598a-f5c3-5cf8-9157-36af83280b75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "934892af-2aea-587f-95fb-a008f0e0a602" :question/math-academy-id "q-94287" :question/problem "A particle of mass $6\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=24\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The governing differential equation is ${y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0$\n- The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f=\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}$\n- The general solution can be expressed as $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $ϕ$ is the phase" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{24}{6}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- The frequency $f$ (measured in ${s}^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{1}{T}=\\frac{\\omega }{2\\pi },\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is the period. So in our case, we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{2}{2\\pi }=\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,.\n$$\n\nSo the frequency $f=\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms.\n\n- $A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nWriting $\\omega =2$ in form 1 gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nHence, statement III is true.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}]}
